              "core_concept": "The 26th Amendment (1971) abolished the privy purses and privileges granted to former princely rulers at integration. The 42nd Amendment (1976), passed during the National Emergency, made the most extensive changes to the Constitution up to that point: it added \"Socialist\", \"Secular\" and \"Integrity\" to the Preamble, inserted Part IVA (Fundamental Duties, Art. 51A) on the recommendation of the Swaran Singh Committee, gave Directive Principles express primacy over some Fundamental Rights, extended Lok Sabha and Assembly terms from five to six years, and curtailed judicial review of constitutional amendments and central laws. The 44th Amendment (1978), enacted after the Congress government's defeat, undid several of these changes: it removed the right to property from Part III and re-enacted it as a mere legal right under the new Art. 300A, restored the five-year term for legislatures, replaced \"internal disturbance\" with \"armed rebellion\" as a ground for proclaiming Emergency under Art. 352, and required the Cabinet's written advice before an Emergency proclamation.",
