              "core_concept": "India adopted the Citizens' Charter concept (modelled on the UK's 1991 Citizen's Charter) at the 1997 Conference of Chief Ministers on 'Effective and Responsive Government'. Charters are administrative instruments, not legally binding or statutorily enforceable in most cases — the key critique of the model. The Department of Administrative Reforms and Public Grievances (DARPG) institutionalised quality standards through the Sevottam framework (2006), an ISO 9001-based model with three components: Citizens' Charter, Public Grievance Redress Mechanism, and Service Delivery Capability. To give charters real teeth, several states have separately enacted statutory Right to Public Services/Guaranteed Service Delivery Acts.",
