              "core_concept": "Family is the first site of moral learning, providing early emotional security and modelled behaviour (primary socialisation). Society — peer groups, culture, religion, media — reinforces or challenges these early values through wider social interaction (secondary socialisation). Educational institutions formalise moral reasoning through curriculum, teacher role-modelling and value education. Lawrence Kohlberg's stages of moral development (pre-conventional, conventional, post-conventional) are the standard psychological framework cited to explain how moral reasoning matures as a person moves through these institutions.",
