              "definition": "This theme covers the core frameworks of major moral philosophers — Indian (Gandhi, Vivekananda, Kautilya) and Western (Aristotle, Kant, Bentham, Mill) — used in GS4 as reference points for analysing ethical dilemmas.",
              "core_concept": "Three broad Western traditions recur: virtue ethics (Aristotle), which locates morality in character and the 'doctrine of the mean' between excess and deficiency; deontological/duty-based ethics (Immanuel Kant), which judges an act right or wrong by its conformity to a universalisable moral law (the categorical imperative) regardless of consequences; and consequentialism/utilitarianism (Jeremy Bentham, refined by John Stuart Mill), which judges acts by their outcomes, aiming at the 'greatest happiness of the greatest number.' Indian traditions add Gandhian ethics (truth, non-violence, and trusteeship — the idea that those who hold wealth or power hold it in trust for society), Kautilya's Arthashastra (rajdharma, tying a ruler's or administrator's legitimacy to subjects' welfare/yogakshema), and Vivekananda's teaching that service to humanity is itself a form of worship.",
